## Chapter 6 \(\Lambda\Lambda\) correlations

### 6.1 \(\Lambda\Lambda\) and \(\bar{\Lambda}\bar{\Lambda}\) HBT results

The statistics for STAR Run 4 is not sufficient to measure the \(\Lambda\Lambda\) and \(\bar{\Lambda}\bar{\Lambda}\) correlation functions. This chapter will give an example of how the future analysis will go.

We have used various cuts in studying \(\Lambda\Lambda\) and \(\bar{\Lambda}\bar{\Lambda}\) HBT effects in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV at STAR. We will present the results with \(\Lambda\) Cuts I and II listed in Tab. 5.1.

The one dimensional correlation function is plotted using the mixed event technique mentioned in Sec. 5.5. Namely, we first select \(\Lambda\) (or \(\bar{\Lambda}\)) candidates by Cuts I or II in each event, then calculate the \(Q\) value for every pair in an event. The \(Q\) distribution for \(\Lambda\Lambda\) (or \(\bar{\Lambda}\bar{\Lambda}\)) pairs is the numerator. To increase statistics, each \(\Lambda\) is mixed with ten other \(\Lambda\)s from different events (or \(\bar{\Lambda}\) with other \(\bar{\Lambda}\)s) and ten \(Q\) values are obtained. This \(Q\) distribution serves as the denominator. The correlationfunction is defined as

\[C(q)=\text{normalization factor}\times\frac{N(q)_{\text{both particles from the same event}}}{N(q)_{\text{pairs from different events}}}.\]

One important detector effect which may alter the measured correlation function is that two particles may be reconstructed as only one track when they are close to each other. This effect is called merged tracks. If there are a lot of merged tracks, then pairs at low \(Q\) are reduced in the same event since particles that are close to each other have higher probability to have low \(Q\), as shown in Fig. 6.1. The pairs in the mixed event sample do not suffer from this problem, hence the measured correlation function may be lower than the real value. To estimate the effect of merged tracks, we plot the distribution of

\[\text{normalization factor}\times\frac{\text{Number of pairs in the same event}(\theta)}{\text{Number of pairs in the mixed event}(\theta)},\]

where \(\theta\) is the opening angle of a pair, with all \(Q\) integrated. The normalized ratio is flat, as shown in Fig. 6.2, thus we do not suffer much from merged tracks in our analysis.

The distribution of the number of \(\Lambda\) and \(\bar{\Lambda}\) per event is shown in Fig. 6.3. On average there are 0.47 \(\Lambda\Lambda\) pair and 0.28 \(\bar{\Lambda}\bar{\Lambda}\) pair per event for Cuts I, 0.55 \(\Lambda\Lambda\) pair and 0.31 \(\bar{\Lambda}\bar{\Lambda}\) pair per event for Cuts II, as shown in Tab. 6.1. The HBT plots are shown in Fig. 6.4. No HBT signal is observed in current data set with these two cuts.

